  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-3\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{\u2218}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{\u2218}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{\u2218}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{\u2218}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{\u2218}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{\u2218}-{45}^{\u2218} \\\\  & ={315}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{\u2218}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{\u2218} \\\\  & ={315}^{\u2218}-{360}^{\u2218} \\\\  & =-{45}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {45}^{\u2218}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$",
